// LEDGER item 132, shape layer (found by the revise round of PR 41; a sibling of item 77). This is the privileged twin of the verifier's program P7.
// A function returns an element of its container PARAMETER through a local identity closure: `fn pick(items, i) { let id = |g| { g }  return id(items[i]) }`, called as `pick([harmless, reader], 1)`.
// The same function over `[hello, world]` (a pure array) is the verifier's P7: master accepts it, and it is REJECTED by this checker with E0507 [all] -- measured cost: the return of the function does not depend on
// the call, and the checker does not carry a container parameter's elements to the call site, so the pure call and this one get one answer. The direct form `return items[i]` is rejected by master too (E0507).
// Master 7cab5799: check rc=0 in default and strict mode, `kryos run` prints TOPSECRET-CLOSURE-9f8e7d6c5b4a (executed); PR 41 head 364d2e44 rejects it (E0507, the read was Unknown).
// The deny! block sits in a `select` arm, which the row layer does not judge (LEDGER item 126, root R4), so the SHAPE layer is the only judge here.
// Must stay REJECTED with E0507 in default and strict mode (the precision work of the revise round must not reopen it), and `kryos run` must not print the secret.
fn harmless() -> str {
    return "not a secret"
}
@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn pick(items: [fn() -> str], i: i64) -> fn() -> str {
    let id = |g: fn() -> str| { g }
    return id(items[i])
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let mut f: fn() -> str = harmless
    let items = [harmless, reader]
    f = pick(items, 1)
    let ch = chan()
    send(ch, 1)
    select {
        v ch => {
            deny!(fs:read) {
                println("OUT: " + f())
            }
        }
    }
}
